WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.759
Film Courage: You said something a moment
ago…”find your tribe.” As someone who

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:07.600
may have felt like an outsider (and I only
say this because I’ve felt like one my entire

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:14.720
life), how do you find your tribe? There’s
a tendency to want a “glob on to,” meaning

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.360
you want to fit in with this group because
you think that it’s is going to take you

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.660
where you need to go, but it’s not really
a fit. How do you know what your tribe is?

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:29.161
Markus Redmond: You don’t. You just don’t.
You just do what you love, man. It’s all

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:34.940
you can do. All you can do is do what you
want to do. That’s how you find them. Who

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:41.559
those people are gonna be…no idea. But you
just have to do what you do. If you do what

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:47.820
they do, then you’re chasing their tribe
and they’re not necessarily your tribe.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.079
So you’re just chasing after people, like
you know, people pleasing, which is something

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:59.120
I’ve had a problem with the past. You can’t
do that. It’s not good for you and it’s

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.719
not good for them. You’re really wasting
everybody’s time when you try to please

00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:09.680
everybody, you know I mean? I think you just
have to do what you do. “Do you” as the

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.700
kids say. Film Courage: That’s interesting.
Why isn’t it good for them? I can see why

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.189
it wouldn’t be good for the individual trying
to be the people pleaser, eventually you’ll

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:25.999
either let them down and they’ll get mad
at you or whatever, but why not for them?

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:32.049
Why does people pleasing hurt the group? Markus:
Why does it hurt the group you’re trying

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:37.659
to please? Well, eventually you can keep it
up. It’s a lie because you’re basically

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:42.829
telling a bunch of people that they can depend
on you to do what they want. It’s not what

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:47.219
you want. You’re gonna crack eventually
and then it’s going to be a waste of their

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:55.499
time and your time. So why do it? Who cares?
You don’t just have to love what you love.

00:01:55.499 --> 00:02:01.609
There’s seven billion people on the planet.
I guarantee at least three of them love what

00:02:01.609 --> 00:02:05.780
you love. That’s all you really need. You
just need to find three other people like

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.720
you, that are good, that like what you like.
And they’re out there. You just have to

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:17.140
do what you do and follow that. The truer
you can be to yourself and what you dig, the

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:22.250
better I think it is for everybody, because
this stuff is hard. It’s not easy to make

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:27.310
movies and all this stuff. You might as well
love it. If you’re gonna do hard stuff,

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:34.010
do hard stuff they’re going to pay you tons
of money for. You know what I mean? Go do

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:39.329
math. Film Courage:&nbsp; Architecture… Markus:&nbsp;Architecture…go
be an architect.&nbsp; That’s hard but you know

00:02:39.329 --> 00:02:47.280
they’ll pay a lot of money upfront, it’s
better. I think you have to do this because

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:53.290
you love it, because it’s burning in you.
Film Courage: So let’s take an example of

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:59.450
someone trying to be a people pleaser as an
actor, filmmaker and then another way, to

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:06.719
not engage in people pleasing, but still be
dependable. Markus:&nbsp; So if you’re an actor

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:13.931
or a filmmaker or both you want to be dependable
to people you end up working but you don’t

00:03:13.931 --> 00:03:18.410
want to be a people pleaser. Film Courage:
Right, what’s an example of each? Markus:&nbsp;

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.440
I think it’s the same thing, just doing
what you love.&nbsp; Because the thing is, if

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:29.269
you love doing something, you’re gonna do
it.&nbsp; People don’t do anything they don’t

00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:35.939
want to do and they only do what they have
to do… Film Courage: Well sometimes for

00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:41.780
a paycheck…
Markus:&nbsp;&nbsp; But they have to do that. So outside

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:46.389
of that,&nbsp; you don’t do anything you don’t
want to do. If you’re doing something you

00:03:46.389 --> 00:03:50.640
don’t want to do, it’s because you have
to do it. Film Courage:&nbsp; How did you catch

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.500
yourself being a people pleaser and how did
you change it? Markus:&nbsp; I’m still doing

00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:01.859
it.&nbsp; You end up in situations where you feel
like you want to work with people.&nbsp; You feel

00:04:01.859 --> 00:04:05.969
like you’re going to gain something out
of that situation.&nbsp; You feel like it’s

00:04:05.969 --> 00:04:10.470
going to be mutually beneficial and sometimes
it is.&nbsp; Sometimes it isn’t and sometimes

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:19.470
you get in there and it’s just not working.&nbsp;
You have to be able to say “Okay, well it’s

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:26.370
not working” You have to take ownership
of your own life.&nbsp; You have to take ownership

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:31.900
of what you’re going to do because what
other people are going to do that’s what

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:40.560
they’re going to do.&nbsp; You can only control
what you are going to do.&nbsp; What you are capable

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:47.199
of, what you love.&nbsp; So you might as well
do this thing you love because you know you’re

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:53.040
going to do it and that makes you dependable.
If you’re going to do it because you love

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.750
it, you’re going to run into the people
that love the same thing and now it’s mutually

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:02.990
beneficial because you’re dependable because
you both love the same thing. But if I come

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:07.379
to you and I say “Hey, yeah…we’re gonna
do this thing, it’s going to be awesome,

00:05:07.379 --> 00:05:11.800
and you’re like ”Yeah, it’s going to
be the best sci-fi horror thing ever and I’m

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:19.610
like yeah! And then I go home and I say, “God,
I hate sci-fi and horror. And I’m like whatever

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:23.639
Karen she know some people and it’s going
to be awesome, so I’m going to keep doing

00:05:23.639 --> 00:05:28.650
it.&nbsp;&nbsp; It’s not going to be the best that
I can do, because I don’t really like it.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.310
And you’re going to be like, “Um….I
don’t think this works and this works and

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:37.560
this works” and I’m going to be like it’s
just sci-fi horror, who the hell cares?&nbsp;

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.650
You just have to kill people in space. What’s
the big deal? And you’re going to be like,

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:48.380
“I love this.”&nbsp; Now you’ve wasted your
time, their time, everybody’s time, you

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:55.919
know what I mean? So you don’t do it. You
just do the thing you think is awesome.&nbsp;

00:05:55.919 --> 00:06:00.120
Not the thing you think is&nbsp; awesome…or
the thing you think other people are going

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.510
to find awesome.&nbsp; Do what you think is awesome
and then the people who think that thing is

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:11.030
awesome will show up, too. Everybody thought
Star Wars was just psycho when they first

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.910
did it.&nbsp; Look at it now…. Question:&nbsp; What
tips can you add on finding your tribe?

